---
import ChapterOpener from "../components/ChapterOpener.astro";
import Pager from "../components/Pager.astro";
import { load, pct } from "../lib/data";

const metrics = load("metrics");
const basel = metrics.basel;
const v99 = metrics.var["0.01"];
const vm = metrics.vol;
const st = metrics.strategies;
const long = metrics.var_long;
const lex = load<any[]>("ablation_default");
const slm = load<any[]>("ablation_ollama");
const base = lex[0].rmse;
const lexWorse = lex.find((r) => r.variant.includes("lexicon")).rmse / base - 1;
const slmWorse = slm.find((r) => r.variant.includes("ollama")).rmse / base - 1;
const meta = load("meta");
---
<section class="chapter" id="risk-factors" aria-labelledby="risk-factors-h">
  <ChapterOpener n="10" title="Risk factors" minutes={3} question="What did not work, and what should you not conclude?" hid="risk-factors-h" />

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      A prospectus puts the risks in writing before anyone invests. These are the results that went
      against me, the bugs I found in my own work, and the limits of the evidence.
    </p>
  </div>

  <ol class="factors">
    <li>
      <h3>News text did not improve the forecast.</h3>
      <p>
        Adding FOMC statements made the volatility forecast worse: {pct(lexWorse)} with the lexicon,
        {" "}{pct(slmWorse)} with the language model. Text is off in every headline result (<a href="#text">chapter 9</a>).
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>A classical model matched the calibration more cheaply.</h3>
      <p>
        Filtered historical simulation was green on {pct(basel.fhs.green)} of days, passed both breach tests at
        99% (Kupiec p {v99.fhs_var.kupiec_p.toFixed(2)}, Christoffersen p {v99.fhs_var.christoffersen_p.toFixed(2)}), and implied
        capital of {pct(basel.fhs.mean_capital)} against {pct(basel.aci.mean_capital)} for adaptive conformal. Conformal's
        advantage is that it needs no model of the return distribution, not that it wins every test.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>The adaptive model's breaches cluster.</h3>
      <p>
        At 99% its breach rate is right ({pct(v99.aci_var.breach_rate, 2)}) but its breaches bunch together
        (Christoffersen p {v99.aci_var.christoffersen_p.toFixed(3)}). It learns from a miss only after it happens,
        so the first days of a shock still get through.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Regime-specific cut-offs over-correct.</h3>
      <p>
        Mondrian conformal lifted stormy-market coverage ({pct(vm.mondrian.by_tercile.high)}) but under-covered calm
        markets ({pct(vm.mondrian.by_tercile.low)}): the volatility levels that define each regime drifted after the
        calibration years.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Risk parity did not help.</h3>
      <p>
        With the same volatility target, equal risk contributions gave a Sharpe ratio of
        {" "}{st.alloc_erc.sharpe.toFixed(2)} against {st.alloc_equal.sharpe.toFixed(2)} for equal weights, while trading {(st.alloc_erc.annual_turnover / st.alloc_equal.annual_turnover).toFixed(1)} times as much
        (annual turnover {st.alloc_erc.annual_turnover.toFixed(1)}× against {st.alloc_equal.annual_turnover.toFixed(1)}×).
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>I found and fixed four bugs in my own pipeline.</h3>
      <p>
        The language-model schema invited sign errors (chapter 9). At 99% the adaptive VaR could become
        infinite after a cluster of misses and stay so for years; a floor on the working level fixed it.
        The metrics had silently dropped those infinite days, flattering the breach rate. And DtACI
        under-covered when outcomes arrive late, until its experts were scored on the levels it had
        actually issued. Each fix has a test.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>The evidence is narrow.</h3>
      <p>
        One portfolio of four funds; one test period, {meta.splits.test.start.slice(0, 4)}–{meta.splits.test.end.slice(0, 4)}, with
        one crash; free daily data from Yahoo Finance; trading costs modelled as a flat
        {" "}{meta.strategy.cost_bps} basis points. The ten-day regulatory test rests on {long.aci.n_nonoverlap} non-overlapping windows,
        too few to separate most models. None of this is investment advice.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <Pager id="risk-factors" />
</section>

<style>
  .factors {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    counter-reset: rf;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
  }
  .factors li {
    counter-increment: rf;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 2.25rem 1fr;
    column-gap: var(--s0);
    padding: var(--s1) 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .factors li::before {
    content: counter(rf) ".";
    grid-row: 1 / span 2;
    color: var(--ink-3);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .factors h3 {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.4;
    margin: 0 0 var(--s-1);
  }
  .factors p {
    grid-column: 2;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
</style>
